        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What are the tidal-flow lanes on the Aston Expressway, and does that affect recovery?<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/></svg></span></summary>
            <p>The Aston Expressway has seven lanes, with the middle three reversible depending on the time of day — mostly inbound towards the city in the morning and outbound in the evening. It's worth telling us the time of day and which lanes were running which way when you called, since that can affect how a recovery vehicle safely reaches you.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Is the Aston Expressway the same road as Spaghetti Junction?<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/></svg></span></summary>
            <p>Not quite — the Aston Expressway is the roughly two-mile stretch of elevated dual carriageway running from Spaghetti Junction (the Gravelly Hill Interchange on the M6) into Birmingham city centre at Old Snow Hill. Spaghetti Junction itself is the interchange at the expressway's northern end.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Do you cover breakdowns right into the city centre end of the expressway?<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/></svg></span></summary>
            <p>Yes — we cover the full length of the A38(M), from Spaghetti Junction down to where it meets the Birmingham Middleway near Old Snow Hill. Give us your direction of travel and the nearest gantry or landmark when you call.</p>
          </details>
